A1. A curb stock. ~ APPLES
A6. A rubber stamp. ~ MARKER
A11. Over half of this country. ~ AMER
A12. In an emergency you can use this for a Ford. ~ SHOAL
A14. Get this out of your head. ~ IDEA
A16. The old Roman scandal. ~ NERO
A17. The Sheik's Chief. ~ SAHIB
A18. A watch word, watch out! ~ TEND
A19. A buffalo making an ass of himself. ~ GNU
A20. Let X equal this and then you can solve it. ~ TEN
A21. Good times are this way now. ~ DUE
A23. This accompanies the sermon. ~ NOD
A24. A person who never offends unintentionally. ~ GENTLEMAN
A27. Like the Prince's yellow necktie. ~ REGAL
A30. A little change for the laundryman. ~ YEN
A31. Any old thing, including husbands. ~ RELIC
A34. Old saws, and dull at that. ~ ADAGES
A36. Tongue-tied like a Senator. ~ FLUENT
A37. This is Greek to a Greek and King George to an Englishman. ~ GR
A38. They clean battleships with this. ~ GOB
A39. Blue Boy. ~ ELI
A40. An old letter Dora thinks is new. ~ NU
A41. These are easy to beat (var.) ~ TABERS
A44. Get this and you're getting ahead. ~ ONWARD
A47. You can stop this with a clothes-pin in a pinch. ~ AROMA
A48. Very rare. ~ RAW
A50. This makes hard roads easy. ~ EASER
A51. The villain of the Wickersham farce. ~ SPEAKEASY
A54. A Maxim silencer. ~ GAT
A57. A blessed event. ~ HAP
A58. A hay-hay shouter. ~ ASS
A59. Undunked. ~ DRY
A61. This got tanned without the sun. ~ ECRU
A63. John McGraw's shortstop is this. ~ GIANT
A65. They come this way from Reno. ~ FREE
A66. This will cause a row. ~ TIER
A67. A Mississippi holdup. ~ LEVEE
A68. A pair of these beats two Aces. ~ PAIR
A69. Attention, Dora! This is dumber than dumb. ~ DENSER
A70. The night watchman's chores. ~ DREAMS

D1. The end of a maiden's prayer. ~ AMEN
D2. The west side of this is all wet. ~ PERU
D3. A man who stands well with all the clubs. ~ PRO
D4. Bertha's birthplace. ~ ESSEN
D5. The widow Murphy's suburban residence. ~ SHANTY
D6. Grandpa's girl friend. ~ MAIDEN
D7. The original comic strip. ~ ALBUM
D8. A carpenter's gladstone. ~ KIT
D9. The old orchard where this apple business started. ~ EDEN
D10. A bisector. ~ RENO
D11. A choice grade of butter. ~ ANGORA
D13. Mr. Henry. ~ OH
D15. This fellow is no quitter. ~ ADDICT
D20. This is what brought stock prices down so fast. ~ TELEGRAPH
D22. This is certainly no fault of Jimmy Walker's. ~ EARLINESS
D24. A snappy story. ~ GAG
D25. Partial support for a chorus girl. ~ LEG
D26. This may lead to neuralgia. ~ NEU
D28. Old Ed. ~ EDGAR
D29. A distant star of great magnitude now visible only in the west. ~ GARBO
D32. When these leap they're Fords. ~ LENAS
D33. This one makes it tough. ~ INURE
D35. This took the air from Rudy Vallee. ~ SOS
D36. Florence, if you know her well. ~ FLO
D41. A Chicago pedestrian. ~ TARGET
D42. Thick letters to the Editor. ~ EMS
D43. Some nut started this. ~ OAK
D45. Here's how! ~ WAY
D46. This guy may take you for a ride. ~ DRAYER
D48. If you trip over this you're stuck. ~ RAPIER
D49. Started drinking in a big way. ~ WEANED
D52. A tenth of a century in gangland. ~ EAGLE
D53. Half a pair of bloomers. ~ ASTER
D55. This is strong and eats almost everything. ~ ACID
D56. This usually leaves in April. ~ TREE
D59. A hot shot. ~ DRAM
D60. These are a cent a dozen. ~ REIS
D62. It takes one of these and a Greek to run a restaurant. ~ URN
D64. A short boulevard. ~ AV
D65. The conductor with a punch (init.) ~ FPA


Warnings:
Note: The answer key ran in the May 9, 1931 issue (page 31, 'Solution of Last Week's Puzzle'). The harvest found only 13 of its 15 columns and didn't match it; the key here is read from the scan.

